<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Gukjagam</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/992">992</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Gukjagam was the national Confucian academy of the Korean kingdom of Goryeo, founded in 992 by King Seongjong in the capital <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaesong">Kaegyong</a>. It trained the sons of officials in the Confucian classics for the civil service. Renamed several times, it became the Seonggyungwan, the highest educational institution of the Joseon dynasty.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>A similar academy, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gukhak">Gukhak</a>, had been set up under Unified Silla but had not succeeded. The Goryeo academy was part of King Seongjong's wider programme of Confucian reform, alongside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gwageo">gwageo</a> civil service examinations and the hyanggyo provincial schools, and it formed the basis of the educational system he envisaged. His edict of 992 gave it land and slaves for its support, but tuition remained too costly for most students not from wealthy families.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>At first there were six courses of study. Three, the Gukjahak, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taehak">Taehak</a> and Samunhak, were reserved for children of the highest-ranking officials; they lasted nine years in all and centred on the Confucian classics. The other three were open to children of officials down to the 8th rank: Seohak (secretarial training), Sanhak (arithmetic) and Yulhak (law), each taking six years, technical training with a heavy admixture of the classics.</p><p>In 1104, in the reign of King Yejong, a seventh division, the Gangyejae, added military training. It is the first recorded occasion of a Korean dynasty providing formal training in the military arts, but tensions between the aristocracy and the military led to its removal from the curriculum in 1133.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the last decades of Goryeo the academy again became a centre of reform through the policies of the early Neo-Confucian scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/an-hyang">An Hyang</a>. In 1304 he levied a tax that officials of the top six grades paid in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> and those below them in cloth, to meet the cost of the students' tuition.</p><p>The academy's name changed several times. It became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gukhak">Gukhak</a> in 1275 on the accession of King Chungnyeol, Seonggyungam in 1298 and Sŏnggyun'gwan in 1308. Under King Gongmin it reverted to Kukchagam in 1358, and in 1362 it took the name Sŏnggyun'gwan again, which it kept until the fall of Goryeo thirty years later.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kukchagam” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Seongjong (r. 981–997) adopted the Confucian programme urged by Choe Seung-ro, created twelve provincial governorships (mok) in 983 and founded the National Academy (<b>Gukjagam</b>) in 992.</blockquote><a href="/empire/goryeo">Goryeo · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
